                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "6a009106-a508-31bc-8892-9bd33fca98ca",
                        "entry_id": "5b189432-eb13-3b07-b661-696cfa2bf647",
                        "glosses": [
                            "chemisches Element mit der Ordnungszahl 118"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "69cb98a8-cad5-4965-a06a-4be392a30e6b",
                                "sense_id": "6a009106-a508-31bc-8892-9bd33fca98ca",
                                "text": "„Durch Alphazerfall zerfällt Oganesson in das Element Livermorium, das jedoch auch in Millisekunden weiter zerfällt.“"
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "65749d27-0603-4c7f-bc47-86325ee8f914",
                        "source_entry_id": "5b189432-eb13-3b07-b661-696cfa2bf647",
                        "source_sense_id": "6a009106-a508-31bc-8892-9bd33fca98ca",
                        "source_sense_description": "chemisches Element mit der Ordnungszahl 118",
                        "target_lang_word": "oganesson",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "oganesson",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "7712b7e4-e487-3478-845b-905552b1ffd2",
                                        "entry_id": "94f563d8-9d2d-3a09-bee1-12b311cb0905",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The chemical element (symbol Og) with atomic number 118. It is the heaviest known element. Highly radioactive, it does not occur naturally. Originally expected to be a noble gas, it is now predicted to be a reactive metallic solid, and a semiconductor or a post-transition metal."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8b839f72-499c-4cea-a7c9-ea77c64eb371",
                                                "sense_id": "7712b7e4-e487-3478-845b-905552b1ffd2",
                                                "text": "SINGAPORE - The four new elements announced in December last year have received their new names: Nihonium (Nh), moscouvium (Mc), tennessine (Ts) and oganesson (Og)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "acca4b0f-7bda-40e1-8f33-46f523558621",
                                                "sense_id": "7712b7e4-e487-3478-845b-905552b1ffd2",
                                                "text": "In January 2016 the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) announced the discovery of four new elements, so completing the seventh row of the periodic table. They have since been named as nihonium, Nh, muscovium^([sic]), Mc, tennessine, Ts, and organesson^([sic]), Og. The procedures for naming new elements are clearly laid out by IUPAC."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f094465b-af5b-41d1-9f0f-30cfa53c51c1",
                                                "sense_id": "7712b7e4-e487-3478-845b-905552b1ffd2",
                                                "text": "Researchers carefully choose the makeup of the beam and the target in hopes of producing a designer atom of the element desired. That’s how the four newest elements were created: nihonium (element 113), moscovium (115), tennessine (117) and oganesson (118) (SN Online: 11/30/16)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "56768432-f3c5-472e-9d8a-c6d1061c3ba2",
                        "source_entry_id": "5b189432-eb13-3b07-b661-696cfa2bf647",
                        "source_sense_id": "6a009106-a508-31bc-8892-9bd33fca98ca",
                        "source_sense_description": "chemisches Element mit der Ordnungszahl 118",
                        "target_lang_word": "oganesson",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "oganesson",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f398fadc-c964-30c5-b4c3-63917b9bb9d3",
                                        "entry_id": "0db22a99-c0f9-38ed-a847-9fea53a49ab8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Nom de l’élément chimique portant le numéro atomique 118 (symbole Og), d’abord synthétisé en octobre 2006. Fin 2015, l’UICPA confirme les découvertes des éléments 113, 115, 117 et 118 et assigne la découverte à la collaboration américano-russe entre l’Institut unifié de recherches nucléaires (JINR) et le Laboratoire national de Lawrence Livermore (LLNL). En juin 2016 ^([1]), l’UICPA propose de le nommer oganesson (symbole Og), nom qui a été entériné en novembre 2016 ^([2])."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3296d93a-b671-4386-ae19-687e35f3f496",
                                                "sense_id": "f398fadc-c964-30c5-b4c3-63917b9bb9d3",
                                                "text": "L’oganesson-294 a une demi-vie d’environ 0,89 milliseconde."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
